      "role": "user",
      "content": "i see. even when you describe their perspective, you're still framing it as being \"driven by a deep-seated racial ideology\" or saying their actions were a \"predictable and inevitable reaction.\" \n\nas a researcher, i'm trying to understand the level of debate that happened at the time. were there any British officials, lawmakers, or even members of the public who actually pushed back against these policies or argued for a more equitable approach to the reserves and land rights? or was the administration's stance pretty much a monolith?",
